                    {
                        id ronin-batched-stat
                        title "Stop issuing one blocking stat per node"
                        sealed true
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-07-31T00:27:29.5972113Z
                                author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                                kind @note
                                text "The largest single finding from ronin-clean-tree-fixture and the only one that reaches the majority of the workload. On the up-to-date tree the no-op is 62% system time — user 3.953 ms against sys 6.402 ms per run — and strace attributes 4,001 of the calls to newfstatat, exactly one per node. Every optimisation in this programme so far moved user-space instructions, which is the other 38%. Stats are issued one at a time and synchronously: RealDiskInterface::stat (src/os.rs:138) calls rustix::fs::statat through a cached directory fd, wrapped in a |path: &Path| disk.stat(path) closure that is threaded as &mut F: FnMut through six call sites in build.rs (666, 686, 756, 885, 1285, 1356) and invoked from nodestat_with (graph.rs:203) in the middle of DirtyEvaluator::evaluate's traversal. Both C samurai and Ninja are serial here too, so this is the one place in the programme where the goal is to beat the reference rather than match it. The restructuring is real: today the traversal decides which node to stat as it walks, and only stats a node whose mtime is_unobserved, so it never pays for a node it does not reach. Batching means collecting the reachable node set first and issuing the stats together — a thread pool, or io_uring on Linux — which trades possibly-wasted stats for parallelism. On a default target covering the whole graph nothing is wasted, which is the common case, but a narrow target would regress unless the batch is seeded from the traversal frontier rather than the whole arena. Everything downstream of the mtimes is already a pure function of them, so once the stats are in hand the existing evaluation is unchanged. Measure against clean-tree-noop specifically; no other fixture will show anything, and note that callgrind understates this entirely because valgrind does not slow syscalls in proportion to instructions — wall time and the user/sys split are the instruments here."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-07-31T00:33:13.863006837Z
                                author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                                kind @note
                                text "Proposed approach: split discovery from stat from evaluation, exploiting an invariant the code already encodes. A stat is a pure function of a path — nothing about it depends on traversal order — while only the dirty evaluation is ordered. And nodestat_with is already guarded by mtime().is_unobserved(), so an evaluation that finds every mtime already recorded performs no syscalls at all. That makes phase three literally today's code, unmodified. Phase one, discovery: walk the graph structure from the target with the existing worklist shape but no stats, collecting the reachable NodeId set into a reused scratch buffer. This is pointer-chasing over the arenas with no syscalls, and because it discovers exactly the reachable set it answers the narrow-target objection recorded above — nothing is stat'ed that the serial version would not have stat'ed. Phase two, batch: add stat_many to the disk interface taking the discovered slice and filling mtimes. Implementation needs no new dependency: std::thread::scope over chunks, sized by std::thread::available_parallelism, each thread calling the existing statat path. RealDiskInterface is already built for this — its doc comment describes sharing the working-directory descriptor across clones, and a raw fd is just a syscall argument, so concurrent statat against it is fine. Phase three: run DirtyEvaluator::evaluate unchanged. Error semantics are the delicate part and there is a clean answer: have the batch record only successful mtimes and leave a failing node unobserved, so phase three stats it again serially and produces the identical GraphError::Stat at the identical point in the traversal. That costs one redundant syscall on a cold path and preserves error ordering exactly, which matters because the conformance oracle compares diagnostics. Gate the whole thing on a node-count threshold — a few hundred — and fall back to the serial path below it, since thread setup would dominate a small graph. Nothing in the dirty logic changes, which is what keeps this reviewable."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-07-31T00:41:14.47068239Z
                                author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                                kind @status
                                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-07-31T00:53:34.49446722Z
                                author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                                kind @decision
                                text "Done, and it is the largest wall-clock result in this programme: clean-tree-noop −22.9, −27.0 and −25.8 percent across three interleaved minimum-of-30 rounds on a quiet 32-core host, taking the workload from 1.10x C samurai to 0.82x — faster than the C reference on the workload where Ronin was second-worst. The design landed as proposed: collect_stat_targets walks the graph structure with no syscalls, RealDiskInterface::stat_many fans the resulting paths across std::thread::scope, and the dirty scan runs unmodified because nodestat_with's is_unobserved guard turns into a hit. Failures are left unrecorded so the scan re-stats them serially and reports the identical GraphError::Stat at the identical point, which is why 425 upstream conformance tests, 10 differential cases and the persistence round trip all still pass. Two things were wrong in the first cut and both are worth recording. The placement was wrong: prefetch_mtimes was inserted immediately before recompute_dirty_with_validations, but add_target runs load_depfiles_for and prepare_build_log_for first, and load_depfiles_for already walks the whole graph stating every source it finds — so by the time the prefetch ran, every mtime was observed and the retain emptied the batch. It measured as exactly neutral and strace showed zero clone3 calls, which is what exposed it; instrumenting the count showed 4,001 collected and 0 surviving the retain. Moving the call to the top of add_target fixed it, and the underlying discovery is worth its own attention: add_target performs three full graph traversals before Plan::add_node makes a fourth. The thread count was also wrong. Splitting 4,001 stats across all 32 cores spends more on clone3 than it saves; scaling instead to one thread per 512 paths gives 7 threads and improves the result from −15/−17 percent to −23/−27. Finally, a measurement warning for whoever revisits this: neither of the instruments this programme has relied on works here. getrusage(RUSAGE_CHILDREN) sums CPU across threads, so it showed sys time rising from 6.402 to 7.750 ms while wall time fell by a quarter, and callgrind likewise counts every thread's instructions — wide-noop reads +4.6% while its wall time is neutral to slightly better. Only interleaved wall clock measures this change."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-07-31T00:54:01.898599892Z
                                author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                                kind @status
                                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                            }
                        )
                    }

