            {
                id ronin-mmap-inputs
                title "Map manifests and logs instead of copying them into buffers"
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-07-31T00:27:57.164722292Z
                        author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                        kind @note
                        text "Endorsed directly by Brendan on 2026-07-31 — \"mmap is always a delicious idea where a read is concerned\". Two sites read whole files into owned buffers that are then only ever borrowed from. parse.rs:501 does std::fs::read on each manifest and hands the Vec to Source, and since ronin-plain-path-fast-path every escape-free path is a Plain(&'source [u8]) borrowed straight out of that buffer — so with a mapping the path bytes would never be copied at all, from disk to interned node. log.rs:168's read_line copies every log line out of a BufRead into a reusable Vec, costing 3.60% of the clean-tree workload, and a mapped log can be split into line slices with no copy whatsoever. The gain is negligible on the current fixtures — a 222 KiB manifest and a 4,000-line log — and material at the scale that motivates the tool: a Chromium build.ninja and its includes run to tens of megabytes, where fs::read is a full copy plus the page faults to touch it. Constraints to respect: Source currently owns its bytes and hands out &'source slices with a lifetime tied to an Arc<Source>, so the mapping must live at least as long as every span derived from it, which the existing Arc already models; a manifest regenerated by its own generator rule mid-build must not be mapped across the regeneration; and mapping introduces SIGBUS on truncation where a read would have returned a short buffer, which matters for a log another process may be rewriting. Prefer a dependency with a maintained safe wrapper over hand-rolled mmap given the crate keeps only three unsafe blocks, all FFI-adjacent. Measure on clean-tree-noop and manifest-command-evaluation, and consider adding a large-manifest fixture, since neither existing shape is big enough to show the win."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-07-31T00:33:37.295221824Z
                        author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                        kind @note
                        text "Proposed approach, and it splits the two files rather than treating them alike, because their risk profiles differ. Manifests: map them. Give Source a private backing enum of Owned(Vec<u8>) and Mapped(..), keep Source::bytes returning &[u8] unchanged, and every caller — including Scanner, which since ronin-scanner-byte-primitive already caches the slice, and the Plain(&'source [u8]) spans from ronin-plain-path-fast-path — is untouched. That is the whole trick: the borrowed-span frontend already did the hard part, so mapping is a change to one constructor. rustix is already a dependency and rustix::mm behind its \"mm\" feature avoids taking on memmap2, at the cost of writing the unsafe block ourselves; given the crate keeps only three unsafe blocks, both FFI-adjacent, either choice is defensible and the dependency is probably the smaller liability. Fall back to fs::read below a size threshold, since mapping a 4 KiB manifest is pure overhead, which also keeps every existing test on the owned path. The log: do not map it. Another ninja or ronin process may be rewriting .ninja_log while we read, and mapping turns a truncation that fs::read would have reported as a short buffer into a SIGBUS we cannot catch. Read it whole into one Vec instead and slice at newlines, which removes read_line's per-line copy — the 3.60% that ronin-log-byte-parsing deliberately left here — with none of the exposure. Preserve MAX_LOG_LINE truncation by capping each slice. The remaining manifest hazard is a generator rule regenerating build.ninja mid-build; ronin already re-reads after that, so the requirement is simply that the old mapping is dropped before the re-read rather than held across it. Measure on clean-tree-noop and manifest-command-evaluation, and expect close to nothing on both — the fixtures are a 222 KiB manifest and a 4,000-line log. This node needs a large-manifest fixture to be judged honestly, which is worth adding first rather than accepting a null result as evidence of nothing."
                    }

