                    {
                        at 2026-07-31T01:06:13.401233579Z
                        author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                        kind @decision
                        text "Split as the design proposed, and the two halves went opposite ways. Mapping manifests is rejected on measurement. The node warned that the existing fixtures are too small to judge it and that a null result there would prove nothing, so a 24.7 MiB manifest was generated first — 200,000 build statements with Chromium-shaped paths, which parses in 910.8 ms. Reading it with std::fs::read costs 6.00 ms, measured directly as a minimum of nine with the page cache warm: 0.66% of the run, and that is the ceiling rather than the saving, because a mapping still faults those pages in when the parser walks them. The premise that this would be material at the scale motivating the tool is simply wrong; parsing dominates so heavily that the read is noise, and the cost — a dependency or a hand-written unsafe block, plus SIGBUS exposure on truncation, plus the mapping-lifetime constraint against a generator rule regenerating the manifest mid-build — buys a fraction of 0.66%. Not worth it at any manifest size this tool will see. The log half was taken and stands on its own, having never depended on mapping: BuildLog::open now reads the file once and borrows each line out of that buffer with split, replacing an incremental reader that copied every line into a scratch Vec first. read_line is deleted. Clean-tree 27,605,504 to 27,125,604 instructions, −1.74%, measured against the immediately preceding commit rather than a stale baseline; read_line's 1,001,113 disappears and BuildLog::open absorbs 543,852 of it. Equivalence was worked through case by case rather than assumed: the header is still compared verbatim so a CRLF log is still rejected, the final split element is skipped because it is either the empty slice after a trailing newline or a line a crash left unterminated — both of which the incremental reader also skipped — and an over-long line is still rejected by parse_entry's MAX_LOG_LINE check. Conformance passes in full, including the log and deps persistence round trip, which is the test that would catch a recovery-semantics change."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-07-31T01:06:37.027466073Z
                        author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id ronin-arena-slot-access
                title "Make arena identifiers carry their own validity"
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-07-31T00:28:04.320525291Z
                        author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                        kind @note
                        text "Endorsed by Brendan on 2026-07-31, with the soundness condition supplied in the same breath: unchecked arena access is defensible only if the identifiers are minted by the graph, so that holding one is itself proof of a valid slot. Today Graph::node, Graph::node_mut, Graph::edge and Graph::edge_mut (src/graph.rs:91-105) index Vec with id.index() and bounds-check every access, and they are the hottest accessors in the program — mknode alone is 5.29% of the clean-tree workload and every traversal, evaluation and side-table update goes through them. The identifiers cannot currently carry that proof: arena_id! exposes a public const from_index(index: usize), so any code can mint a NodeId for a slot that does not exist, and the type says nothing about which Graph it belongs to. Making the guarantee real means narrowing minting to the arenas themselves — from_index becomes private to the graph module, or is replaced by a push that returns the identifier it just created — and, if the stronger property is wanted, branding the identifier to a particular graph instance so an identifier from one arena cannot index another, which is the generativity or GhostCell pattern. Only once minting is closed does get_unchecked stop being a hope. Note what is already true and load-bearing: the arenas are append-only, so an identifier is never invalidated by later growth, and that half of the invariant needs no work. Order matters — close the minting first and measure that alone, since narrowing from_index is a pure refactor with a real chance of catching a latent bug, and only then consider the unchecked access as a separate, separately measured step. Two callers legitimately reconstruct identifiers from side-table indices (build.rs, in command_edge_count and the wanted-edge filter) and will need a sanctioned route rather than the general constructor."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-07-31T00:33:48.233803097Z
                        author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                        kind @note
                        text "Proposed approach, in two separately measured steps, because only the first is a prerequisite for soundness and it has value on its own. Step one, close the minting. arena_id! generates a pub(crate) const from_index, and there are 42 call sites outside util.rs and the tests, so today any code in the crate can conjure an identifier for a slot that does not exist. Move the identifier types into a module that owns them and narrow the constructor to pub(in crate::graph), leaving index() crate-visible since side tables legitimately need it. Then audit each of the 42: most are inside the arenas and stay; the ones outside need a sanctioned route. The pattern for the two in build.rs — command_edge_count and the wanted-edge filter, both enumerating self.wanted, which is parallel to the edge arena — is graph.edge_ids().zip(&self.wanted), which drops from_index entirely and continues the direction ronin-edge-id-iteration already took. Expect this step to be instruction-neutral; its value is that it makes the invariant true rather than merely believed, and an audit of 42 sites has a fair chance of surfacing something. Step two, and only after step one lands: replace the indexing in Graph::node, node_mut, edge and edge_mut with get_unchecked behind a SAFETY comment that cites the closed constructor and the append-only arenas — the second half of the invariant is already true and needs no work, since growth never invalidates an existing identifier. Measure that step alone; mknode is 5.29% of the clean-tree workload and every traversal and side-table update goes through these four accessors, but bounds checks are also exactly what a branch predictor eats for breakfast, so treat the estimate as unknown. Considered and rejected: branding identifiers to a graph instance with a lifetime parameter, the generativity or GhostCell pattern. It is the stronger property and it would catch cross-graph misuse, which tests do construct, but it puts a lifetime on every identifier and every signature that touches one. The cost is not proportionate when closed minting plus append-only arenas already give the invariant that get_unchecked actually needs."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-07-31T00:54:04.597964656Z
                        author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-07-31T00:59:05.482092179Z
                        author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                        kind @decision
                        text "Step one done; step two deliberately not attempted, and the reason is a genuine soundness gap rather than a time constraint. arena_id! now takes a visibility for its minting constructor, defaulting to pub(crate) so the env and names arenas are unchanged, and NodeId and EdgeId narrow theirs to pub(in crate::graph). Every fabrication site outside the graph module is gone: grep for NodeId::from_index or EdgeId::from_index outside src/graph now returns nothing. The four external sites were converted as the design proposed. Plan::wanted_edges and Plan::command_edge_count walked side tables that run parallel to the edge arena and rebuilt identifiers from the enumeration index; both now zip the arena's own edge_ids alongside, which also removed an enumerate. BuildLog::hydrate_runtime took a Range<usize> and minted a NodeId per index; it now takes impl Iterator<Item = NodeId>, and the one partial-range caller — synchronize_runtime, hydrating only nodes the runtime just grew into — passes graph.node_ids().skip(nodes.start), so the identifiers come from the arena that grew. The event loop was the interesting case: it encodes an EdgeId into a poll key and decodes it back, which is a legitimate round-trip of an identifier the caller already held rather than an assertion that a slot exists, so EdgeId gained an explicit event_key and from_event_key pair defined in graph.rs beside the arena. Measured neutral to marginally better, wide no-op 23,809,505 to 23,647,251 instructions, as the node predicted; the value was always the invariant rather than the speed. Now the reason step two stops here: from_event_key is pub(crate) and will build an EdgeId from any integer, so it remains a fabrication route, and the supervisor decodes keys that come back from the polling layer rather than from a value it can prove it holds. That is fine while Graph::edge bounds-checks — a bogus identifier panics — but it is exactly the case that would become undefined behaviour under get_unchecked. Closing it properly means either validating the decoded key against the arena at the boundary, which the supervisor cannot do because it has no graph, or keeping the identifier in a side table the supervisor owns rather than round-tripping it through an integer. Until one of those lands, unchecked indexing is not justified, and the honest position is that this step narrowed the minting surface from crate-wide to the arena plus one documented round-trip without yet reaching a proof. 367 tests pass, clippy clean, conformance green."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-07-31T01:01:39.576429995Z
                        author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id ronin-large-manifest-gap
                title "Act on what the 25k-to-200k edge measurements exposed"
                children (
                    {
                        id ronin-eager-stat-storm
                        title "Stop stating the whole graph before reporting a missing input"
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-07-31T11:27:48.990692754Z
                                author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                                kind @note
                                text "A real defect, found while investigating something else. On a 200,000-edge manifest whose sources are absent, ronin issues 400,001 newfstatat calls — every one returning ENOENT — before reporting that the first source is missing; samurai issues 3 and reports the same error. Both then exit. It predates the batched-stat work: ronin-prestat, the binary from before that change, makes the identical 400,001 calls, so this is not something the prefetch introduced. The cause is ordering. Builder::add_target runs load_depfiles_for first, which walks the whole reachable graph and stats every generator-less node at build.rs:670 to set its dirty bit, and only afterwards does Plan::add_node discover that one of those nodes is both dirty and ungenerated and raise BuildError::MissingInput. Samurai instead stats as it plans and aborts on the first failure. Note carefully what this does and does not cost: on a build that succeeds every one of those stats is needed anyway, so there is no waste in the common case. The cost is confined to the error path, where a user with one missing source waits for a full stat sweep — measured at roughly 400 ms of the 1.08 s run on this fixture — before being told. Proposed approach: load_depfiles_for already computes dirty as mtime().is_missing() for exactly these nodes at build.rs:672, so the detection can happen there rather than a traversal later. The obstacle is not detection but diagnostics. BuildError::MissingInput carries a needed_by field that names the consuming node, which load_depfiles_for's traversal does not track, and the message text and the order in which competing errors surface are part of the Ninja compatibility surface that the pinned conformance suite compares. So the work is: thread the consumer through that traversal, then verify against the oracle that the first-reported missing input is the same node as before. Measure the error path specifically, since no existing workload exercises it, and do not expect any change on a successful build."
                            }
                        )
                    }

                    {
                        id ronin-stat-batch-cpu-cost
                        title "Spend fewer cores for the batched stat's wall-clock win"
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-07-31T11:27:58.956212616Z
                                author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                                kind @note
                                text "The batched stat landed in a35c91d buys real wall time but at a CPU cost that was never measured, because wall clock was the only instrument used and it hides this entirely. On the 200,000-edge fixture perf reports the current binary at 3,091 ms task-clock across 2.858 CPUs, against ronin-prestat at 1,395 ms across 0.990 — so 23% of wall time (1.409 s to 1.082 s) costs 2.2 times the CPU and 2.0 times the cycles (11.08 G against 5.46 G), with instructions per cycle collapsing from 0.98 to 0.44. Nothing about that is visible in the wall-clock rounds the node was accepted on. The IPC collapse points at contention rather than useful work: 32 threads issuing failing path lookups against the same directory descriptor serialize in the kernel on shared dentry and inode state, so past some width the threads mostly wait for each other. This matters beyond tidiness. A build tool spends its cores on compilation, and under -j the prefetch is taking cores the user wants elsewhere; on a shared or containerised machine it is simply rude. Proposed approach: measure the width curve rather than guess it. PER_THREAD is currently 512, which on 400,001 paths yields the full 32 threads; sweep it so that width lands at 2, 4, 8, 16 and 32 and record both wall time and task-clock at each, then choose the knee rather than the minimum wall time. Expect the knee to be low, since the first measurement of this change used all 32 cores and scored −15 to −17 percent while 7 threads scored −23 to −27 percent — more threads were already worse, and the curve was never followed further down. Add task-clock to whatever the harness records for this workload, because a change that halves wall time while quadrupling CPU should not be able to pass unremarked again."
                            }
                        )
                    }

                    {
                        id ronin-large-manifest-fixture
                        title "Benchmark a manifest larger than four thousand statements"
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-07-31T11:28:14.769352618Z
                                author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                                kind @note
                                text "Every workload in the suite is at most 4,001 build statements, so scale is a blind spot in exactly the way dirtiness was before ronin-clean-tree-fixture. That matters because ronin's advantage over C samurai is not constant across size: measured on parse-and-evaluate, 0.83x at 4,000 edges, 0.89x at 25,000, 0.94x at 50,000, 0.99x at 100,000 and 1.06x at 200,000. Every claim this programme has made about beating the C reference is therefore true only at the size the fixtures happen to be, and the crossover sits inside the range real projects occupy — Chromium's manifest is far past 200,000 statements. Proposed approach: add a workload at 100,000 statements, which is 12 MiB of manifest and about 300 ms per tool, rather than the 200,000 used for the ad-hoc measurement, since the harness generates a fresh copy per tool and generation plus three runs at 200,000 costs several seconds per repetition. Give it Chromium-shaped paths — roughly forty characters, nested a few directories deep, with a bounded number of distinct directories so the interner sees realistic reuse — because path length is a live hypothesis for the erosion and the existing fixtures use ten-character paths. Run it through the commands tool so it measures parsing and evaluation without stats or execution, which keeps it a clean scaling probe and avoids the trap that invalidated the ad-hoc fixture, where absent sources made both tools take an error path they handle very differently. Record it as an addition rather than a version bump, as clean-tree-noop was: existing shapes stay byte-identical and the CSVs key on workload name. Watch the harness runtime; if generating three 12 MiB manifests per invocation proves too slow for the release gate, the fixture belongs in a separate scaling report rather than the default catalog."
                            }
                        )
                    }

                    {
                        id ronin-node-locality-at-scale
                        title "Find why per-edge cost grows faster than samurai's"
                        deps (ronin-large-manifest-fixture)
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-07-31T11:28:24.724439063Z
                                author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                                kind @note
                                text "Ronin's per-edge cost grows with graph size faster than samurai's. Measured on parse-and-evaluate: ronin goes 1.95, 2.44, 2.67, 2.96 and 3.15 microseconds per edge at 4,000, 25,000, 50,000, 100,000 and 200,000 statements, a rise of 62%, while samurai goes 2.35, 2.75, 2.83, 2.99 and 2.97, a rise of 26% that is essentially flat past 50,000. Both tools grow, which is expected once working sets exceed cache, but ronin grows more than twice as fast, and that is what turns a 17% lead at fixture size into a 6% deficit at 200,000. Instruction counts do not explain it — the shape is characteristic of locality — so this needs measuring as a memory problem rather than an instruction-count one. Proposed approach: with perf now available, start with cache-references, cache-misses, dTLB-load-misses and instructions-per-cycle across the size sweep for both tools, and see whether ronin's miss rate diverges where the per-edge curves do. If it does, the first suspect is node path storage. ronin-path-interning-arena is marked Done but its own description set an endgame of a single append-only byte arena with nodes holding u32 offset and length spans, and Node::path is still a BString — an individually heap-allocated buffer per node — so what landed was the shellpath and duplicate-key parts, not the arena. Four hundred thousand separate allocations walked in traversal order is exactly the shape that degrades with size while a contiguous arena would not, so confirm that reading of what shipped before proposing work, then judge the arena against measured miss rates rather than against the assumption. Depends on ronin-large-manifest-fixture: without a suite workload at this size there is nothing to hold the result against, and the ad-hoc fixtures used here are not reproducible."
                            }
                        )
                    }
                )
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-07-31T11:27:33.1385048Z
                        author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                        kind @note
                        text "Findings from measuring 25,000 to 200,000-edge manifests on 2026-07-31, and one methodology correction that matters more than any of them. Callgrind has been the primary instrument for this whole programme and it is a user-space simulator that does not count kernel instructions. On a 200,000-edge fixture it reported ronin 3,074,971,466 against samurai 2,729,279,505, a ratio of 1.13, while perf reported 5,331,534,241 against 2,939,783,556, a ratio of 1.81 — the entire difference being kernel instructions ronin executes and samurai does not. Any conclusion this programme drew from callgrind about syscall-adjacent work is unsound, and the batched-stat node already recorded that callgrind and getrusage both mislead on threaded work. Use perf for anything touching syscalls, callgrind only for pure user-space comparisons, and interleaved wall clock as the arbiter. Getting perf working needed kernel.perf_event_paranoid lowered from Debian's default of 3 to 1, which also wants kernel.kptr_restrict at 0 for kernel symbols. A retraction belongs here too: an earlier claim that ronin runs 1.7x slower than C samurai at every realistic project size was wrong, and wrong because of a fixture defect of my own making. That fixture declared 200,000 sources that did not exist on disk, so both tools error out — but samurai discovers the missing input during its plan walk and bails after 3 stat calls while ronin stats 400,001 first, so the comparison measured one tool short-circuiting against the other doing full work. Re-measured on parse-and-evaluate through the commands tool, which both tools complete, the real curve is 0.83x at 4,000 edges, 0.89x at 25,000, 0.94x at 50,000, 0.99x at 100,000 and 1.06x at 200,000 — a genuine but gentle erosion from seventeen percent faster to six percent slower across a fifty-fold size range, not a constant 1.7x. Disk-side caching of the parsed graph was considered and dropped without a node: profiling the large run puts the genuinely cacheable work — scanstring, canonpath, append_variable and parse — at about 27%, while mknode, wide_hash, node_for and the allocator, which together are more, would simply become deserialization. A load-free mapped arena format could do better, but at that point a resident daemon dominates it, which is what Bazel and Buck2 do."
                    }
                )
            }
        )
        log (
            {
                at 2026-07-29T22:58:22.604396347Z
                author "brendan@bbqsrc.net"
                kind @note
                text "Follow-up programme for the eleven findings from the full blind Rust review. Preserve Ninja compatibility, the byte-exact and dense-arena decisions, and the pinned conformance oracle. Existing compatibility rules remain owned by the shipped nodes; each leaf starts as target-impl scaffolding and must gain or revise internal specification rules before implementation when it introduces a normative safety, error, or performance guarantee."
            }
        )
    }

